<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Simon bar Giora</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 71 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Simon bar Giora was a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-jewish-roman-war">First Jewish–Roman War</a> who commanded one of the rival factions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> during the Roman siege of 70 CE. After the city fell he was captured, led in the triumph of Vespasian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-saint">Titus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and executed. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> portrays him as a ruthless tyrant.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>His name means Simon, son of Giora, and Giora is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> for convert or sojourner; most scholars today agree that his father was a Gentile convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>. Josephus calls him a Gerasene by origin. Which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerash">Gerasa</a> he meant is debated: Gerasa in the Decapolis is one possibility, but given Simon's later activity in Acrabatene and around Bethoron, a place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a> is generally thought more likely.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 66 he led attacks on the Roman army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cestius-gallus">Cestius Gallus</a> as it approached Beth Horon, throwing its rear into disorder and carrying off pack animals with its weapons. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> authorities, hoping to moderate the revolt, refused him a command, and he turned to raiding wealthy houses in Acrabbene before taking refuge with the rebels at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masada">Masada</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ananus-ben-ananus">Ananus ben Ananus</a> was killed, he left the fortress, proclaimed liberty for slaves and gathered a large following, sacking villages in Idumea and plundering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebron">Hebron</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zealots">Zealots</a> in Jerusalem captured his wife, he mutilated captives taken outside the city until she was released.</p><p>In spring 69 the Roman advance drove him into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, where the remaining authorities invited him in to drive out John of Giscala. With fifteen thousand soldiers he held the upper city and part of the lower, living in the tower of Phasael. The factions destroyed each other's grain stores in a sabbatical year, when grain was already short. He ordered the execution of the High Priest Matthias ben Boethus and three of his sons as suspected friends of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> wrote that he was a greater terror to the people than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> themselves.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-saint">Titus</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 70, five months after the siege began, Simon hid in passages beneath the Temple Mount and tried to dig his way out until his food ran out. Dressed in the garments of a Judean king, he rose from the ground where the Temple had stood and was taken prisoner. He was held in the Mamertine Prison, paraded in chains through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the triumph, tortured and executed in the Forum near the Temple of Jupiter. Cassius Dio calls him the leader of the Judeans.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Simon bar Giora” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jerusalem then split between rival factions under John of Gischala, <b>Simon bar Giora</b> and Eleazar ben Simon, who fought each other and burned food stores.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-judea">Provisional Government of Judea</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Gerasa, in what is now <a href="/countries/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.8,33&amp;year=61" aria-label="Gerasa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5387 904 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Simon bar Giora"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5837 1174h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5837 1174h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.8,33&amp;year=61">What was here in 61 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
